        <p>Notice that the calculation of average velocity does not depend on the path taken by the particle.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Instantaneous velocity</h2>
        <p>
            The velocity of a particle at time {`\\(t\\)`} whose position vector {`\\(\\vec{r}\\)`} is given as a function of time is defined as {`\\[\\vec{v} = \\frac{d\\vec{r}}{dt}\\]`} Usually, this computation is done by first resolving vector {`\\(\\vec{r} = x\\hat{i} + y\\hat{j}\\)`} and then differentiating {`\\(x\\)`} and {`\\(y\\)`} with respect to time. That is, {`\\[\\vec{v} = \\frac{dx}{dt}\\hat{i} + \\frac{dy}{dx}\\hat{j} = v_x \\hat{i} + v_y\\hat{j}\\]`}
        </p>

        <p>
            Notice that {`\\(\\vert d\\vec{r}\\vert = ds\\)`} where {`\\(ds\\)`} is distance travelled in time {`\\(dt\\)`}. Hence, the magnitude of instantaneous velocity is equal to instantaneous speed. {`\\[v = \\Big\\vert\\frac{d\\vec{r}}{dt}\\Big\\vert = \\frac{\\vert d\\vec{r}\\vert}{dt}=\\frac{ds}{dt}\\]`} Unlike average velocity, instantaneous velocity depends on the path taken by the particle.
        </p>
